---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const breakevenCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'p*x', label: 'revenue = price × units', color: '#c2410c', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: 'f + v*x', label: 'total cost = fixed + variable × units', color: '#374151' },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'p', label: 'price p', min: 5, max: 100, step: 1, value: 40 },
    { name: 'v', label: 'variable cost per unit v', min: 1, max: 80, step: 1, value: 25 },
    { name: 'f', label: 'fixed costs f', min: 0, max: 6000, step: 50, value: 2400 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 500, label: 'units sold' },
  crossings: true,
  height: 360,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/foundational/unit-economics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every business, from a tea stall to a trillion-dollar platform, lives or dies on one calculation:
    does a <strong>unit</strong> of what you sell — one cup, one subscription, one ride — bring in more
    money than it costs to deliver? This is the subject called <strong>operations management</strong>:
    the craft of turning inputs (materials, people, machines, ideas) into outputs (goods and services)
    at a cost the market will pay. Its core vocabulary is cost. <strong>Fixed costs</strong> are owed
    regardless of output — rent, salaries, insurance, the loan payment on the espresso machine; they
    are the price of existing. <strong>Variable costs</strong> scale with each unit — the coffee, the
    cup, the delivery fee. Revenue rises with price and volume. Where the revenue and total-cost lines
    cross is the <strong>break-even point</strong> — the sales you must make before the business earns
    anything at all. Everything else in this lesson — how production is organised, how scale changes
    costs, what quality really costs, where to locate — is an attempt to move that crossing point left,
    or the profit gap right.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the circled crossing is survival — drag price, costs and see exactly how many units it takes" widget="plotter" config={breakevenCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the widget as a worked example with defaults: price £40, variable cost £25, fixed costs
    £2,400. Each unit contributes £40 − £25 = <strong>£15</strong> (the <em>contribution margin</em>)
    toward fixed costs, so break-even is 2,400 ÷ 15 = <strong>160 units</strong> — the crossing point.
    Try the experiments: raise the price p and the break-even point slides left (fewer sales needed —
    but will customers stay?). Cut variable cost v and the gap between the lines widens — every unit
    after break-even earns the gap as profit. Double the fixed costs f and the whole cost line rises:
    this is why "high-fixed-cost" businesses (airlines, chip fabs, cinema chains) live or die on
    volume. One more number matters in practice: the <strong>margin of safety</strong> — how far current
    sales sit above break-even. Selling 240 units against a 160-unit break-even is a 33% cushion; a
    15% demand dip survives it, a 40% dip does not.
  </p>

  <h2>Production: goods and services, and how they're made</h2>
  <p>
    Operations first chooses a <strong>method of production</strong>, and the choice shapes everything
    downstream. <strong>Job production</strong> makes one-offs — a wedding cake, a bespoke suit, a
    bridge: maximal customisation, high unit cost, skilled labour, no economies of scale.
    <strong>Batch production</strong> makes groups — a bakery's 200 rye loaves before switching to
    sourdough: some variety, some scale, but every switch costs setup time. <strong>Flow (mass)
    production</strong> runs identical units down a continuous line — bottled drinks, cars: lowest unit
    cost, but inflexible and exposed to any line stoppage. Services version the same spectrum (a
    bespoke consultancy is job production; a coffee chain is flow production with milk). The second
    choice is <strong>labour-intensive versus capital-intensive</strong>: hand-tailoring versus an
    automated bottling line differ in fixed/variable mix exactly as the widget's lines do — capital
    intensity raises fixed costs and lowers variable cost per unit, moving break-even right and making
    profits explosive at high volume, fragile at low volume. The third is <strong>productivity</strong>:
    output per unit of input. Raising productivity without raising quality is the operational
    holy grail, and the modern answer is <strong>lean production</strong> — the Toyota-inspired system
    that attacks all waste (<em>muda</em>): just-in-time inventory (hold days of parts, not months —
    freeing cash and warehouse space, at the cost of vulnerability to supply shocks, as the pandemic
    demonstrated), <strong>kaizen</strong> (continuous small improvements proposed by the people doing
    the work), and error-proofing so defects never restart a process. Lean's insight is accounting
    disguised as philosophy: inventory is money sitting still, and time is a cost line.
  </p>

  <h2>Scale: why bigger is usually cheaper — until it isn't</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Economies of scale</strong> are the fall in cost per unit as output rises, and they are
    specific enough to list. <em>Purchasing</em>: a chain buys flour at wholesale prices a stall cannot
    touch. <em>Technical</em>: a large machine or factory spreads its fixed cost over more units — the
    high-fixed-cost logic of the widget. <em>Marketing</em>: a national advertising campaign costs the
    same and sells a hundred times more. <em>Financial</em>: large firms borrow at lower rates.
    <em>Managerial</em>: a specialist accountant or logistics manager, unaffordable for a small firm,
    pays for itself at scale. These are why scale is the recurring dream of every competitive industry.
    But <strong>diseconomies of scale</strong> are just as real: coordination costs grow with size,
    communication slows through layers, workers feel anonymous, and the firm becomes slow precisely
    when the market is fast — cost per unit can <em>rise</em> past some size. The practical measure
    tying scale to the break-even picture is <strong>capacity utilisation</strong>: actual output as a
    percentage of maximum output. A factory running at 60% is paying full fixed costs while earning
    revenue on 60% of capacity — high utilisation is how the fixed-cost line gets amortised, which is
    why airlines discount last-minute seats (a near-zero-variable-cost seat flown empty earns nothing;
    sold cheap, it contributes something) and why utilisation is watched obsessively in hotels,
    shipping and semiconductors.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> economies of scale do not come from "being big" vaguely — they
  come from spreading fixed costs and buying inputs cheaper, both of which are visible in the two cost
  lines of the widget. A business can be huge and still have no scale economies (a big restaurant
  chain's locations each repeat the same small-scale costs), and a small firm can enjoy purchasing
  economies by joining a buying cooperative. Ask which line moved, not how big the firm is.</p>

  <h2>Operating leverage: the widget's hidden risk dial</h2>
  <p>
    The cost structure on the widget's two lines also determines how violently profit responds to
    sales — a property called <strong>operating leverage</strong>. Work it with the defaults
    (price £40, variable £25, fixed £2,400): at 240 units, profit is 240 × 15 − 2,400 = £1,200. Lift
    sales 25% to 300 units: profit becomes 300 × 15 − 2,400 = £2,100 — a 75% jump. The same 25% move
    downward (to 180 units) takes profit from £1,200 to £300 — a 75% collapse. High fixed costs
    <em>amplify</em> everything, both directions: the identical percentage sales swing that barely
    dents a low-fixed-cost stall (whose profit is nearly proportional to volume) whipsaws the
    capital-intensive factory. This is the quantitative content behind the earlier claim that
    high-fixed-cost businesses "live or die on volume" — airlines at 75% load factors scrape by and
    at 95% print money, in exactly this ratio. It also explains strategic behaviour: high-leverage
    firms cut prices to defend volume during downturns (any price above variable cost is still
    contributing), while low-leverage firms protect margin instead. When you read the widget, you
    are reading not just where profit begins but how much turbulence the model can absorb.
  </p>

  <h2>Quality: the cheapest cost reduction</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Quality</strong> is conformance to specification — the product does what it is supposed to
    do, reliably — and its economics are badly intuited. The naive view is that quality costs money.
    The operational view is the reverse: the <em>cost of poor quality</em> is a bill with four parts —
    prevention (training, good design, error-proofing), appraisal (inspection and testing), internal
    failure (scrap, rework, downtime), and external failure (returns, warranty claims, refunds, and the
    unquantifiable one: reputation). External failure is the catastrophic tier because it multiplies —
    a defect caught at the bench costs parts; the same defect in a customer's hands costs recall,
    litigation and trust. This arithmetic drives the shift from <strong>quality control</strong>
    (inspect the finished output, catch defects — necessarily some escape) to <strong>quality
    assurance</strong> (design the process so defects cannot be made), and to <strong>total quality
    management</strong> (TQM): quality as everyone's job, every process measured and continuously
    improved, the customer — internal or external — as the final judge of every handoff. The
    measurable payoff loops straight back into unit economics: less scrap lowers variable cost per
    unit, fewer warranty claims lower the fixed service burden, and a reliability reputation raises
    the price the market will bear — the same break-even diagram, moved on both axes.
  </p>

  <h2>Location: the decision that fixes the cost lines</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Location decisions</strong> are operations in concrete: where the cost and revenue lines
    are set for a decade. The classic factor checklist: proximity to <em>materials</em> (weight-losing
    industries — steel, sugar — sit near the raw input; hauling ore to the coast for centuries), to
    <em>energy</em> (why aluminium smelters chase cheap electricity), to <em>labour</em> (skills and
    wage levels — the gravitational force behind manufacturing's 20th-century migrations), to
    <em>the market</em> (weight-gaining and perishable products — soft drinks, bread — are made near
    the customer), plus infrastructure, planning permission, taxes and grants. Modern overlays change
    the weighting: e-commerce makes a distribution centre's position relative to courier hubs more
    important than a shopfront's footfall; cloud services relocate "offices" into data-centre
    geography chosen for power and latency; remote work lets service firms hire labour without moving
    it. And relocation is asymmetric — moving a factory to cut labour cost 20% can quietly raise
    logistics cost 15%, lose embedded supplier relationships, and disrupt the workforce that held the
    tacit knowledge; firms that count only the wage line in the location decision routinely rediscover
    the others at their own expense.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The break-even model earns its keep only if you know its edges. It assumes a <em>constant</em> price
    and constant variable cost per unit — but real firms face learning curves (unit variable cost falls
    with cumulative output) and price discrimination (the airline seat sold at ten prices), so the
    straight lines bend; multi-product firms cannot even define "the" unit cleanly, which is why
    practice computes break-even per product line on <strong>contribution</strong> and sums.
    Contribution logic also powers short-run decisions: a hotel room unsold tonight is revenue lost
    forever (services are perishable — you cannot inventory an empty room), so any price above the
    variable cost of cleaning it contributes something — the mathematics behind last-minute discounts,
    which puzzles people until they see the fixed costs are sunk regardless. The startup
    reinterpretation is <strong>customer lifetime value (LTV) versus customer acquisition cost
    (CAC)</strong>: the "unit" is a customer, the "variable cost" includes what it costs to acquire
    them, and the business is viable only if LTV > CAC with margin to spare — "growth at a loss" is
    rational <em>only</em> if today's subsidised price builds tomorrow's positive unit economics, the
    discipline behind blitzscaling and the epitaph on most startup post-mortems when it wasn't true.
    Finally, cash still outranks profit: a profitable firm dies of bad timing when salaries fall due
    before customers pay (working capital), which is why the break-even chart sits next to a cash-flow
    forecast on every competent founder's desk. Operations, in the end, is the discipline of making
    the widget's two lines behave: revenue up, variable cost down, fixed cost amortised — and quality,
    lean production, scale and location are just the four levers, labelled.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Horngren's Cost Accounting (Datar & Rajan); Ohno, Toyota Production System (1988); Osterwalder & Pigneur, Business Model Generation; standard IGCSE/A-level business operations syllabus treatments.</p>
</TopicPage>
